\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 The Golden Rule of Invoicing: "Snapshotting"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Because you aren't tracking inventory, you might be tempted to just type descriptions and prices directly into the 
\f2\fs26 invoice_items
\f1\fs24  table every time. That works fine!\
However, if you have standard services that you sell repeatedly (like a "$150/hour consulting fee"), you should create a master 
\f3\b\fs26 services
\f1\b0\fs24  or 
\f3\b\fs26 products
\f1\b0\fs24  table to hold those standard rates.\
When a user creates an invoice, you pull the price from the 
\f2\fs26 services
\f1\fs24  table, but 
\f0\b you must copy and save that price directly into the 
\f3\fs26 invoice_items
\f0\fs24  row.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u9888 \u65039  
\f0\b Why?
\f1\b0  Because if you change your consulting rate from $150 to $200 next year, your historical invoices must not change. If you only link to a master price table without taking a "snapshot" of the price at the time of purchase, all your past financial records will retroactively update and ruin your accounting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

